# ADR-0019: Formalize repository Skill workflow contracts

## Context

The repository-owned `sdd-change` and `sdd-requirements` Skills already contain
trace annotations for two session-scoped requirements and two design
components. Those targets were never added to normative artifacts because the
Skill files were accidentally excluded from this repository's trace inputs.
Correcting input selection exposes the missing targets as strict-trace
failures.

The underlying workflow behavior is already intentional: each new development
request begins with fresh requirements and change scope unless continuation is
explicit, and implementation requires current validated and approved
requirements and design.

## Decision

Formalize the existing workflow contracts as
`REQ-SESSION-SCOPED-DEVELOPMENT-001`,
`REQ-SESSION-SCOPED-DEVELOPMENT-002`,
`DES-SESSION-SCOPED-DEVELOPMENT-001`, and
`DES-SESSION-SCOPED-DEVELOPMENT-002`.

Keep the existing Skill code node identities and bind them directly to the new
normative targets. Verify both requirements with one trace-annotated contract
test that reads the authoritative Skill files and asserts each obligation
against the file that owns it. Do not create proxy code or test nodes merely to
satisfy trace coverage.

## Rejected alternatives

- Remove the existing Skill annotations: rejected because the workflow files
  are repository-owned implementation and should participate in traceability.
- Bind the Skill nodes to broad existing lifecycle requirements: rejected
  because those requirements govern runtime evidence, not Copilot development
  session behavior.
- Leave the targets undefined and tolerate dangling edges: rejected because
  strict trace requires zero dangling endpoints and the constitution forbids
  claiming complete evidence with trace errors.
- Duplicate the Skill text into a TypeScript proxy: rejected because it would
  make a non-authoritative copy appear to implement the workflow.

## Consequences

Strict trace can include the real Skill code nodes without dangling requirement
or design targets. Future changes to the session-scoped workflow obligations
must update the normative requirements, design, Skill text, and contract test
together.

The contract test verifies textual workflow invariants rather than executing a
Copilot session, so it proves the repository-owned instruction contract, not
external agent compliance.

